Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Reliquary**, a tall, precious monument that cradles a
glowing solar core, encircled by a turning orbital ring and crowned by counter-rotating
guardian fins — as a 60x100x60 opaque-voxel model using only the `voxel-anim` tool. You
build it by **painting discrete opaque cells** and you **author its ring, core, and fin
animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief tells
you *what the Reliquary is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how you
break the monument into a fixed body and its moving pieces, where you place the joints,
and how you shape every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a
self-cycling reliquary needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. It is the sole channel: you build the model by calling it **one operation
at a time**, and the ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to
`parts/<part>.actions.json` — together with the rig you define are the **authoritative
output that is scored**, not the voxels on disk. Anything produced another way (writing
files directly, editing a preview by hand) is discarded and flagged; only the recorded
operations and the rig count.

You paint the geometry as **discrete opaque cells**: `voxel-anim` sets and clears single
voxels, fills and strokes boxes, draws 3D lines and spheres, and mirrors across a plane
— each cell an opaque `#rrggbb` color; there is **no transparency**. Every voxel
operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is sculpted **separately**
in its own preview and log, in the shared volume's coordinates. The volume starts
**empty**.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each a
parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the required
animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation targets a part
with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it. After each call the
binary re-renders that part's isometric preview to `parts/<part>.png` and appends the
operation to that part's log.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name plinth
voxel-anim fill-box --part plinth --x0 8 --y0 0 --z0 8 --x1 51 --y1 52 --z1 51 --color "#c69a4b"
voxel-anim add-sphere --part solar_core --cx 30 --cy 56 --cz 30 --r 8 --color "#ff9d2e"
```

(The part names above are just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every voxel operation **and** the rig and animation
subcommands (defining parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring animations
with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`parts/<part>.png` after your calls** to see that part's progress, **and read the
assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`,
`scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole monument composed from all your parts,
re-rendered after every operation. The scene views are how you confirm the parts
actually fit together: that the core sits cradled at the monument's heart, the ring
encircles it, and the fins crown it. Because each part is sculpted in the shared
volume's coordinates, position each part where it belongs on the finished monument; the
scene simply composes them there.

